> Working on the 3MG3 and 5UI2 protein made me understand somethign important: i need to 
find a way to deal with ligands the correct way. 
> This requires changes in both the hbonds in flexibility and the hbondpipe part

Wokring on other examples, i have come across three different issues:
> unknown ligands and how to deal with them (5ui2)
	- This became apparent when i was running hbondpipe and it wasn't produceing the hbondfile 
	- quick fix for now:  
	alertign the hbondpipe scrict to stripping HETATM records for H-bond detection: in creates a pdb 
	just with the porotein and then run phenix.hbonds on that
	- Note: phenix.reduce was running normally, didn't seem to be afftected from the unknown ligand
	- But for this particular protein  the ligand is EQ3, from what i learn it is a hydrophobic ligand, so it 
	more possible ot intereact with the VdW. But this is now the general case of course, so at some points the ligands need to be addressed. 
> Gap in the chain (3mg3)
	- the code was hanging indefinitely when processing H-bonds for 3MG3.
	- 3MG3 has a gap in its sequence between residues 164 and 170 (confirmed by manual PDB inspection and grep "^TER"). 
	- This gap causes ROPE to build two separate kinematic trees within chain A:
		> Tree 1: residues 1–164 (root at block 1)
		> Tree 2: residues 170–end (root at block 2408, A-LYS170:N)
		> When lastCommonAncestorIdx() tried to find a common ancestor for an H-bond spanning these two trees, it got stuck in an infinite loop because:
			- Block 2408 has parent_idx = 0 and depth = 0 — it's a root with no parent
			- The while(true) loop had no exit condition for this case
		The Fix:
			1. Exit condition in lastCommonAncestorIdx(): detect when either block reaches a root (parent_idx == 0 and depth == 0) without finding a common ancestor, return empty vector and log a warning
			2.  Track and report how many H-bonds were skipped due to no common ancestor, alongside the existing inter-chain and missing atom counters
		Still open: 
			-  the gap at residues 165–169 means H-bonds crossing that region are excluded from the Jacobian, likely overestimating flexibility around that loop. Need to discuss whether this is acceptable or whether the gap needs to be modelled.
			- same problem will appear on ligands, need more permanent solution
> 1bb5: 
	- hydrogens were reportred with names that ROPE couldn't foudn: replace them with H

